Let the plane be $\mathcal{P}$. The normal vector to the plane $-2x + y - 3\zeta = 7$ is $\mathbf{n} = \begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 1 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix}.$  Since $\mathcal{P}$ is parallel to the aforementioned plane, it admits the identical normal vector, and consequently, it is expressible as
\begin{equation} \label{eq:plane} \langle \mathbf{x} - \mathbf{p}, \mathbf{n} \rangle = 0 \end{equation}
where $\mathbf{p} \in \mathcal{P}.$  The specific instantiation of $\mathbf{p}$ entails $\mathbf{p} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 4 \\ -2 \end{pmatrix}.$  Consequently,
\begin{align*} \begin{pmatrix} x - 1 \\ y - 4 \\ z + 2 \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 1 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix} &= 0 \\ -2(x - 1) + (y - 4) - 3(z + 2) &= 0 \\ -2x + 2 + y - 4 - 3z - 6 &= 0 \\ -2x + y - 3z - 8 &= 0 \\ 2x - y + 3z + 8 &= 0 \end{align*}
Therefore, the equation of the plane is $2x - y + 3z + 8 = 0.$

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{2x - y + 3z + 8 = 0}$

