Let $f(x) = \cot \frac{x}{4} - \cot x$ be valid for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$ devoid of singular points. Namely, $x \neq \pi n$ for all $n \in \mathbb{Z}$, and $\frac{x}{4} \neq \pi m$ for all $m \in \mathbb{Z}$.

We seek the identification between $f(x)$ and the prescribed parametrization.

\begin{align*} \label{eq:1} f(x) &= \cot \frac{x}{4} - \cot x \\ &= \frac{\cos \frac{x}{4}}{\sin \frac{x}{4}} - \frac{\cos x}{\sin x} \\ &= \frac{\cos \frac{x}{4} \sin x - \cos x \sin \frac{x}{4}}{\sin \frac{x}{4} \sin x} \\ &= \frac{\sin (x - \frac{x}{4})}{\sin \frac{x}{4} \sin x} \\ &= \frac{\sin \frac{3x}{4}}{\sin \frac{x}{4} \sin x} \end{align*}

Thus, the requisite form is satisfied with $k = \frac{3}{4}$.

