response:
Let $\mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}$ and $\Pi$ denote the aforementioned Euclidean plane. The projection of $\mathbf{v}$ upon $\Pi$ is given by
\begin{align*} \mathcal{P}(\mathbf{v}) := \left( \mathbf{v} - \frac{\langle \mathbf{v}, \mathbf{n} \rangle}{\| \mathbf{n} \|^2} \mathbf{n} \right) \mathcal{R}(\mathbf{n}). \end{align*}
where $\mathbf{n} \in \Pi$ satisfies $\mathbf{n} \not\equiv \mathbf{0}.$  We can take $\mathbf{n} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -1 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix}.$  Then
\begin{align*} \mathbf{v} \cdot \mathbf{n} &= \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -1 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix} = 1 \cdot 3 + 2 \cdot (-1) + 3 \cdot 4 = 3 - 2 + 12 = 13. \\ \|\mathbf{n}\|^2 &= 3^2 + (-1)^2 + 4^2 = 9 + 1 + 16 = 26. \end{align*}Therefore,
\begin{align*} \text{proj}_{\mathbf{n}} \mathbf{v} &= \frac{\mathbf{v} \cdot \mathbf{n}}{\|\mathbf{n}\|^2} \mathbf{n} \\ &= \frac{13}{26} \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -1 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \frac{1}{2} \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -1 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} 3/2 \\ -1/2 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}. \end{align*}
The projection of $\mathbf{v}$ onto the plane $3x - y + 4z = 0$ is
\begin{align*} \mathbf{v} - \text{proj}_{\mathbf{n}} \mathbf{v} &= \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix} 3/2 \\ -1/2 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} 1 - 3/2 \\ 2 - (-1/2) \\ 3 - 2 \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} -1/2 \\ 5/2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}. \end{align*}
Thus, the projection of $\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}$ onto the plane $3x - y + 4z = 0$ is $\begin{pmatrix} -1/2 \\ 5/2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}.$

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{\begin{pmatrix} -1/2 \\ 5/2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}}$

