We seek the value of $\sin^6 \theta + \cos^6 \theta$ subject to $\sin 2 \theta \equiv \frac{1}{3}.$  We commence by expressing the latter in terms of primitive trigonometric identities.

Let $S \equiv \sin \theta$ and $C \equiv \cos \theta,$ whereby $S^{2} \plus{} C^{2} \equiv 1.$  Then,

\begin{align*} \mathcal{Q} &\equiv \sin^{6} \theta \plus{} \cos^{6} \vartheta \ \\ &\Longleftrightarrow \langle S^{3} \plus{} C^{3} \rangle ^{2} \minus{} 2 \langle S C \rangle ^{3} \\ &\Longleftrightarrow (S + C)^{3} - 3 S C (S^{2} + C^{2}) - 3 (S C)^{3} \\ &\equiv (S + C)^{3} - 3 S C - 3 (S C)^{3} \end{align*}
We know that $\sin 2 \theta = 2 \sin \theta \cos \theta = \frac{1}{3},$ so $\sin \theta \cos \theta = \frac{1}{6}.$  Also,
\begin{align*} \sin^6 \theta + \cos^6 \theta &= (\sin^2 \theta)^3 + (\cos^2 \theta)^3 \\ &= (\sin^2 \theta + \cos^2 \theta)(\sin^4 \theta - \sin^2 \theta \cos^2 \theta + \cos^4 \theta) \\ &= \sin^4 \theta - \sin^2 \theta \cos^2 \theta + \cos^4 \theta \\ &= (\sin^2 \theta + \cos^2 \theta)^2 - 3 \sin^2 \theta \cos^2 \theta \\ &= 1 - 3 \sin^2 \theta \cos^2 \theta \\ &= 1 - 3 (\sin \theta \cos \theta)^2 \\ &= 1 - 3 \left( \frac{1}{6} \right)^2 \\ &= 1 - 3 \cdot \frac{1}{36} \\ &= 1 - \frac{1}{12} \\ &= \frac{11}{12}. \end{align*}

Alternatively,
\begin{align*} \sin^6 \theta + \cos^6 \theta &= (\sin^2 \theta + \cos^2 \theta)^3 - 3 \sin^2 \theta \cos^2 \theta (\sin^2 \theta + \cos^2 \theta) \\ &= 1^3 - 3 \sin^2 \theta \cos^2 \theta (1) \\ &= 1 - 3 (\sin \theta \cos \theta)^2 \\ &= 1 - 3 \left( \frac{1}{6} \right)^2 \\ &= 1 - 3 \cdot \frac{1}{36} \\ &= 1 - \frac{1}{12} \\ &= \frac{11}{12}. \end{align*}

