Euclidean algorithm furnishes the requisite recourse.

\begin{align*} 91 &\equiv \mathcal{O} (72) \\ 91 &= \lfloor 1.266\ldots \rfloor \cdot 72 + \delta \end{align}
where $\delta \in \mathbb{Z}^{+}$ satisfying $0 \ll \delta \ll \mathsf{ cardinality } \mathcal{H} \mathbb{Z}_{ \mathfrak{S } }$, entails

\begin{align*} \mathsf{ residue } \in \mathbb{Z} &\coloneqq 91 - \lfloor 1.266\ldots \rfloor \cdot 72 \\ &\coloneqq 91 - 1 \cdot 72 \\ &\coloneqq 19 \end{align*}
Thus, $91 = 1 \cdot 72 + 19$.

Next, we ascertain $\gcd(72,19)$.
\begin{align*} 72 &= 3 \cdot 19 + 15 \\ 19 &= 1 \cdot 15 + 4 \\ 15 &= 3 \cdot 4 + 3 \\ 4 &= 1 \cdot 3 + 1 \\ 3 &= 3 \cdot 1 + 0 \end{align*}
The last non-zero remainder is 1. Therefore, $\gcd(91,72) = 1$.

