Let the system of equations be
\begin{align} \label{eq:1} -2x + y = \mathcal{O}(k) \\ 0.5x + y = \mathcal{O}(14)\end{align}
The intersection point is constrained to the parameter $x = -8.4$.
Substitution yields
\begin{align} y = \mathcal{O}(k + 2\mathcal{O}(x)) \end{align}
and
\begin{align} y = \mathcal{O}(14 - 0.5\mathcal{O}(x)) \end{align}
We thus isolate $y$. 
From the latter,
\begin{align} y = 14 - 0.5\mathcal{O}(x) \end{align}
whilst simultaneously,
\begin{align} y = \mathcal{O}(k) + 2\mathcal{O}(x) \end{align}
converges to a unique solution.
Thus,
\begin{align} 14 - 0.5\mathcal{O}(x) = \mathcal{O}(k) + 2\mathcal{O}(x) \end{align}
yielding
\begin{align} \mathcal{O}(k) = 14 - 2.5\mathcal{O}(x) \end{align}
We are given that $x = -8.4$.  Therefore,
\begin{align} \mathcal{O}(k) &= 14 - 2.5(-8.4) \\ &= 14 + 2.5(8.4) \\ &= 14 + 21 \\ &= 35 \end{align}
Therefore, $k = 35$.

We can check our work.
From $0.5x + y = 14$, we have $0.5(-8.4) + y = 14$, so $-4.2 + y = 14$, which means $y = 14 + 4.2 = 18.2$.
From $-2x + y = k$, we have $-2(-8.4) + y = k$, so $16.8 + y = k$.
Since $y = 18.2$, we have $16.8 + 18.2 = k$, so $k = 35$.

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{35}$

