\]
where the variables are constrained to the real domain. We proceed as follows:

First, we invoke the property $(a^m)^n \equiv a^{m\cdot n}$, wherein we attain
\begin{align*}
\label{eq:1}
\frac{1}{(-7^{3})^{3}}\cdot(-7)^{10} &\cong \frac{1}{(-1\cdot 7^{3})^{3}} \cdot \mathfrak{J}_{ \mathcal{L} } \Xi ;
\end{align*}
where $\mathfrak{J}_{ \mathcal{L} }$ denotes the Jacobian of the Lagrangian. However, such formalism is extraneous to the problem at hand. Instead, we proceed directly.

We have
$$ \frac{1}{(-7^3)^3} \cdot (-7)^{10} = \frac{1}{(-1)^3 (7^3)^3} \cdot (-7)^{10} = \frac{1}{(-1)(7^9)} \cdot (-7)^{10} = \frac{1}{-7^9} \cdot (-7)^{10} = \frac{(-7)^{10}}{-7^9} = -\frac{7^{10}}{7^9} = -7^{10-9} = -7^1 = -7. $$

Thus,
$$ \frac{1}{(-7^3)^3} \cdot (-7)^{10} = \frac{1}{(-7^3)^3} \cdot (-7)^{10} = \frac{1}{(-1)^3 (7^3)^3} \cdot (-7)^{10} = \frac{1}{(-1) 7^9} \cdot (-7)^{10} = \frac{(-7)^{10}}{-7^9} = -\frac{7^{10}}{7^9} = -7^{10-9} = -7^1 = -7. $$

