Let $n$ be an integer such that $n \equiv -1 \pmod{50}$. In other words, $n = 50\zeta - 1$ for some integer $\zeta$. We seek an evaluation of $n^2 + 2n + 3 \operatorname{mod} 50$.

Recourse to the aforementioned congruence yields

\begin{align*} n^2 + 2n + 3 &\equiv (-1)^2 + 2(-1) + 3 \operatorname{mod} 50 \\ &\equiv 1 - 2 + 3 \operatorname{mod} 50 \\ &\equiv 2 \operatorname{mod} 50 \end{align*}
Thus, $n^2+2n+3$ leaves a remainder of 2 when divided by 50.

Alternatively, since $n$ is $1$ less than a multiple of $50$, we can write $n = 50k - 1$ for some integer $k$. Then
\begin{align*} n^2+2n+3 &= (50k-1)^2 + 2(50k-1) + 3 \\ &= (2500k^2 - 100k + 1) + (100k - 2) + 3 \\ &= 2500k^2 - 100k + 1 + 100k - 2 + 1 \\ &= 2500k^2 \end{align*}
The last expression is a multiple of $50$, wherefore the congruence relation $n^2+2n+3 \operatorname{mod} 50$ obtains the solution of $2$
\boxed{2}

