In our instance, the coordinates $(10,3)$ furnish
\begin{align*} r &\equiv \sqrt{10^{2} + 3^{2}} \approx 10.488 \\ \theta &\equiv \arctan\left(\frac{3}{10}\right) \approx 0.291 \end{align}
whereupon the latter is constrained to the requisite quadrant.

Consider the transformation of coordinates from $(R,\psi)$ to $(X,Y)$ whereby
\begin{align*} X &= R\cos(2\psi) \\ Y &= R\sin(2\psi) \end{align*}
Accordingly, substituting $R=r^{2}$ and $\psi=2\theta$ yields
\begin{align*} X &= r^{2}\cos(4\theta) \\ Y &= r^{2}\sin(4\theta) \end{align}
whereupon the latter invariants remain.

The Cartesian coordinate of the target is induced by
\begin{align*} X &\equiv \mathcal{R} \in \mathbb{R} \\ Y &\equiv \mathfrak{I} \in \mathbb{complex} \end{align}
which furnishes
\begin{align*} \langle \mathfrak{X}, \mathfrak{Y} \rangle &\cong \mathbb{S}^{4} \end{algorithmic}

Given the antecedent, viz. the instantiation of the coordinate manifold, we may recursively decompose the resultant whereby
\begin{align*} X &= r^{2}\cos(2\theta) \\ Y &= r^{2}\sin(2\theta) \end{align*}
We have $r = \sqrt{10^2 + 3^2} = \sqrt{100+9} = \sqrt{109}$ and $\theta = \arctan \frac{3}{10}$.
Then
\begin{align*} X &= r^2 \cos(2\theta) = (\sqrt{109})^2 \cos(2\theta) = 109 \cos(2\theta) \\ Y &= r^2 \sin(2\theta) = (\sqrt{109})^2 \sin(2\theta) = 109 \sin(2\theta) \end{align*}
Since $\theta = \arctan \frac{3}{10}$, we have $\cos \theta = \frac{10}{\sqrt{109}}$ and $\sin \theta = \frac{3}{\sqrt{109}}$.
Then
\begin{align*} \cos 2\theta &= \cos^2 \theta - \sin^2 \theta = \frac{100}{109} - \frac{9}{109} = \frac{91}{109} \\ \sin 2\theta &= 2 \sin \theta \cos \theta = 2 \cdot \frac{3}{\sqrt{109}} \cdot \frac{10}{\sqrt{109}} = \frac{60}{109} \end{align*}
Thus,
\begin{align*} X &= 109 \cos 2\theta = 109 \cdot \frac{91}{109} = 91 \\ Y &= 109 \sin 2\theta = 109 \cdot \frac{60}{109} = 60 \end{align*}
Therefore, the rectangular coordinates of the point with polar coordinates $(r^2, 2\theta)$ are $(91, 60)$.

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{(91,60)}$

