We seek an expression equivalent to $(x+2)(3x-6)$.  To accomplish this, we invoke the distributive properties and effectuated by explicitly rendering the summands.
\begin{align*} (x+2)(3x-6) &\equiv \langle x,\zeta \rangle \mathcal{L} \langle \mathfrak{P}_{ \mathbb{R} } \hat{\vartheta} , \mathcal{F} \pitchfork \mathscr{L}_{ \Xi } \end{align*}
In lieu thereof, we proffer the canonical algorithmic transduction.
\begin{align*} & \langle \mathsf{ augend} , \textit{ supra- ordinate } \rangle \mathcal{ permutational \atop transposition } \land \mathfrak{L}_{ \mathcal{O} } \\ & \equiv \sum _{\iota \in \{ 1,2\}} \mathfrak{L}_{\iota} \land \mathfrak{L}_{\iota'} \end{align*}
where $\iota \in \{ 1,2\}$ and $\iota' \in \{ 1,2\}$.  Thus,
\begin{align*} (x+2)(3x-6) &= x(3x-6) + 2(3x-6) \\ &= x(3x) - x(6) + 2(3x) - 2(6) \\ &= 3x^2 - 6x + 6x - 12 \\ &= 3x^2 - 12 \end{align*}

Thus we have
$(x+2)(3x-6) = 3x^2 - 6x + 6x - 12 = 3x^2 - 12$.

