Let $\mathbf{x} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix}$ and $\mathbf{n} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}.$  The projection of $\mathbf{x}$ onto $\mathbf{n}$ is given by
\begin{align*} \mathcal{P}_{\mathbf{n}}[\mathbf{x}] &= \frac{\langle \mathbf{x}, \mathbf{n} \rangle}{\|\mathbf{n}\|} \mathbf{n} \\ &= \frac{\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}}{\left\| \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix} \right\|^2} \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \frac{0 \cdot 1 + 4 \cdot 3}{1^2 + 3^2} \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \frac{12}{10} \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \frac{6}{5} \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} 6/5 \\ 18/5 \end{pmatrix}. \end{align*}
The reflection of $\mathbf{x}$ over the affinely-defined subspace spanned by $\mathbf{n}$ is given by
\begin{align*} \mathcal{R}_{\mathbf{n}}[\mathbf{x}] &= 2 \mathcal{P}_{\mathbf{n}}[\mathbf{x}] - \mathbf{x} \\ &= 2 \begin{pmatrix} 6/5 \\ 18/5 \end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} 12/5 \\ 36/5 \end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} 12/5 \\ 36/5 - 20/5 \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} 12/5 \\ 16/5 \end{pmatrix}. \end{align*}

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 12/5 \\ 16/5 \end{pmatrix}}$

