\begin{align*} \sec \left( -300^\circ \right) \equiv \frac{1}{\cos \left( -300^\circ \right)} \end{align*}
further substantiated via the Euler theorem wherein parity dictates
\begin{align*} \cos \left( - \varsigma \right) \Longleftrightarrow \Re \left( e^{ i \varsigma } \right) \end{align}
whereupon implication furnishes
\begin{align*} \Re \left( e^{- i 300^\circ} \right) \end{align*}
however, a more facile recourse entails leveraging the periodicity of trigonometric functions. Specifically, the cosine function satisfies $\cos(\theta + 360^\circ) = \cos(\theta)$. Thus,
\[ \cos(-300^\circ) = \cos(-300^\circ + 360^\circ) = \cos(60^\circ) = \frac{1}{2}. \]
therefore
\begin{align*} \sec \left( -300^\circ \right) \Longleftrightarrow \frac{1}{\cos \left( -300^\circ \right)} \approx \frac{1}{0.5} \Longrightarrow \mathcal{R} \mathbb{Z}^{+}\cap\mathbb{Q} \end{align}
